		<h2 style="text-align: center;">Career Support</h2>
		<h2 style="text-align: center;">College of Frontline Supervision, Management, and Leadership (CFSM)</h2>

        <p>Professionals are busy and have many things to do. DirectCourse supports learners to gain the skills they need at their own pace. It helps professionals grow in their job.</p> 
            
        <p>DirectCourse products are part of development. They work best when blended with other ways of learning. This includes on-the-job training, coaching, and supervision. It may also include instructor-led learning. The <strong>Resources</strong> section of each lesson’s menu has many tools. These can support blended learning and independent study.</p>

  		<h3>Competency Development and Assessment</h3>
        <p>Competencies are the knowledge, skills, and attitudes that professionals need to do their jobs. DirectCourse helps learners reach high levels of competency. It also supports learners to earn meaningful credentials or skill validation (such as CEUs). DirectCourse does this by:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Building from validated competency sets aligned with recognized credentials and best practices. (See below.)</li>
            <li>Identifying lesson objectives and tying them to the competencies.</li>
            <li>Designating a practice level for each lesson. These align with common approaches to career development. (See below.)</li>
            <li>Providing many ways to practice and assess progress including:</li>
                <ul>
                    <li>A post-test for learners after each lesson. A score of 80% or more is needed to pass.</li>
                    <li>Practice activities with feedback.</li>
                    <li>On-the-job descriptions of which lesson competencies are being applied. These can be used to assess skills. They can be the basis for on-the-job and just-in-time training.</li>
                    <li>Reflection activities and useful tools. These can be used as part of facilitated discussions in group training. Learner responses can also be shared with a supervisor or trainer.</li>
                    <li>Examples of how to develop work samples that show competence. These can be used for portfolio development or <a href="https://ebadge.nadsp.org" target="_blank">NADSP E-Badge demonstrations</a>.</li>
                </ul>
        </ul>
        <p>More information about how these connect to specific lessons is found in the <strong>Resources</strong> section of the lesson.</p>

        <h2>Credentialing and Credit for Learning</h2>
        <p>Learners may want or need to earn specific credits or a credential in some roles. DirectCourse content is tied to credentials in the following ways:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>NADSP: All DirectCourse content is accredited by the <a href="https://nadsp.org/" target="_blank">National Alliance for Direct Support Professionals (NADSP)</a>. It counts toward education hours for the <a href="https://ebadge.nadsp.org/" target="_blank">NADSP E-badge Academy</a> program. In addition, many states and providers use the <a href="https://nadsp.org/services/certification/" target="_blank">NADSP Certification</a> as a credential for DSPs. Contact the NADSP to learn more.</li>
            <li>Certificates: Learners can download a certificate of completion for each lesson finished. (See below to learn how to do this.) Certificates have the details that licensing and credentialing boards usually require. They can be sent to boards for CEU approval. Check directly with your credential or licensing board to see what is needed. Contact your DirectCourse learning administrator if you need additional information.</li>
        </ul>


        <h3>How to Obtain a Certificate of Completion</h3>
        <p>DirectCourse lesson-level certificates provide the most commonly requested information for continuing education (CE) hours. To obtain a certificate, do the following:</p>
        
        <ol>
            <li>Complete the lesson. Take the 10-question final test. A score of 80% or higher is needed to receive a certificate. Tests may be taken again as needed. The highest score will remain on a transcript.</li>
            <li>After passing the test, exit and re-enter the lesson. Find and click the certificate option at the top of the <strong>Lesson Information</strong> page. Complete the lesson evaluation. The certificate will then open as a PDF file. This can be saved or printed.</li>
        </ol>


        <h3>Competency Sets Used in CFSM</h3>
        <p><strong>CFSM</strong> CFSM uses the following competency sets as the basis for developing content. There is more information in the <strong>Resources</strong> section about specific competencies for each lesson.</p>
        
        <p>The <a href="https://rtc.umn.edu/docs/NationalFrontlineSupervisorComp.pdf" target="_blank">National Frontline Supervisor Competencies (NFSC)</a>. These are foundational competencies that include the key knowledge, skills, and abilities for supervising DSPs. They also have competencies related to effective service planning and support coordination. These competencies reflect best practices in the field. The NADSP uses these competencies as the basis for their frontline supervisor (FLS) credential.</p>

        <p>The <a href="https://www.ltsscenter.org/resource-library/Mid_Level_Manager_Competency_Development_Guide.pdf" target="_blank">LeadingAge Mid-Level Manager Core Competencies</a>. These are key skills for mid-level managers in long-term services and supports (LTSS). National experts created this competency set.</p>

        <p>The <a href="https://thenadd.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/Specialist-Program-Manual-Revised-6.28.23.pdf" target="_blank">NADD Competencies for the Dual Diagnosis Specialist (DDS) Certification</a>. These competencies were developed by experts in program development for people with intellectual and developmental disabilities and mental health needs. DDS manage, train, and supervise DSPs. They build programs in organizations that support people with these needs.</p>

        <h3>Practice Levels in CFSM</h3>
        <p>Each lesson in this product has a designated practice level. This is meant to help training directors and learners think about how content may fit into a learner’s career development and competency. Practice levels are based on the skills expected at certain points of tenure and experience. Keep in mind, each position is unique. Practice levels are only intended as guidelines to help with building unique training plans. Content at any level can be used as it makes sense for overall training goals.</p>

        <p>The following practice levels are used in this content:</p>

        <p><strong>Entry (E)</strong>: Entry content covers common core skills needed in the first three months by service planners or supervisors.  </p>
        <p><strong>Intermediate (I)</strong>: Intermediate content focuses on common core skills to develop by the end of the first year for service planners and supervisors.</p>
        <p><strong>Management (M)</strong>: Management content focuses on skills needed in mid-level roles. These lessons include content on program planning, direction, and policy development. Learners build skills for developing and implementing organizational practices.  </p>
        <p><strong>Leadership (L)</strong>: Leadership content is for anyone who wants to learn more about leadership. Content has a focus on how national, state, or local policy shapes services and supports. It also focuses on how to lead others.  </p>
        <p><strong>Specialty (S)</strong>: Specialty content explores the needs of specific populations or requirements of specific types of services. They are matched with the other practice levels. </p>

